-- title:
--   Parity and exponent of admissible transports of a rigidification
-- statement:
--   Fix distinct primes $r$, $\bar r$ and a non-zero level $N$ with $r \nmid N$. The arithmetic data are: a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain $\mathcal{O}$ with irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adically complete, whose residue ring has exactly $r$ elements and in which $(r) = (\pi)$, with fraction field $K_0$; and a characteristic-zero $\mathcal{O}$-algebra domain $O^{nr}$ with an $\mathcal{O}$-automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element integral over $\mathcal{O}$ modulo $\pi$, every monic polynomial of positive degree having a root modulo $\pi$, and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r$ modulo $\pi$. The quaternionic data are: rationals $a,b$ with $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ indefinite and ramified exactly at $r$ and $\bar r$, a maximal order $\Lambda$ containing $\mathbb{Z}$, an order coordinate map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \times \mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$, a fake elliptic curve $A_0$ of level $N$ over $O^{nr}/(\pi)$ with two-dimensional formal coordinates $\theta_0$ presenting it as a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module via $\mathrm{coord}$ and a height-$4$ module $X_0$. The moduli-theoretic data are: $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{r^2} \to O^{nr}$, a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ of height $4$ over $O^{nr}/(r)$, a moduli package $M$ over $O^{nr}$ which is a Zariski sheaf together with a family $\eta$ of maps from rigidified objects to $M$-points which separates admissible objects exactly up to isomorphism, commutes with base change and is Zariski-locally surjective, an injective embedding $E_0$ of the centraliser of the $\mathbb{Z}_{r^2}$-action and $\varpi$ on $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ whose image agrees with $M_2(\mathcal{O})$ up to a fixed power of $r$, the reduction $\kappa : O^{nr}/(\pi) \to O^{nr}/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps, and an isogeny $\beta_0$ of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to $X_0$ pushed along $\kappa$; these are summarised here. Over a non-zero connected Noetherian $\mathcal{O}$-algebra $B$ (no idempotents other than $0,1$) in which $\pi$ and $r$ are nilpotent, with $\psi : O^{nr} \to B$, let $x$ be a fake elliptic curve over $B$ together with a rigidification relative to $A_0$, let $(X,\theta)$ present that curve as a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module via $\mathrm{coord}$, and let $t = (X, t.n, t.\rho)$ and $t_2 = (X, t_2.n, t_2.\rho)$ be rigidified objects over $B$ with the same underlying $X$, each a transport of the rigidification with exponents $j$ and $j_2$ (so that the respective $\rho$ is the reduction of a representing series composed with $\beta_0$ and with $X_i \mapsto X_i^{r^{j}}$, resp. $X_i^{r^{j_2}}$), and each admissible for the twisted structure map $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$, resp. $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j_2}$, i.e. $X$ special of height $4$ and $\rho$ an isogeny of height $4\,t.n$, resp. $4\,t_2.n$. Then $j \equiv j_2 \pmod 2$ and $2\,t_2.n + j = 2\,t.n + j_2$.
--
--   This is the compatibility between the exponent of a Frobenius twist and the isogeny level of an admissible transport, in the supersingular dictionary underlying the Čerednik–Drinfeld description of a Shimura curve: the invariant $2n + j$ depends only on the rigidified curve, and $j$ is determined modulo $2$. It is used to show that transports along $\mathrm{Fr}^{-1}$ shift $n$ in the expected way, and in the construction of the scaling action on the associated moduli points.
